    <div class="hbadge rv in">Journal · Nature</div>
    <h1 class="rv in" style="font-size:clamp(2.2rem,5vw,4rem);max-width:20ch">Madeira or the Azores: Which Is Better for Whales and Boat Trips?</h1>
    <div class="artmeta rv in d1"><span>1 January 2027</span><span>6 min read</span><span>Funchal · Madeira</span></div>
  </div>
</header>

<section class="pad">
  <div class="wrap">
    <article class="article rv">
      <p class="lede">For whales, and big whales above all, the Azores have the edge: sperm whales are there all year, blue, fin and sei whales pass mostly from March to May, and lookouts on land guide the boats. Madeira is warmer, quicker to reach from mainland Europe, has 28 confirmed whale and dolphin species of its own, and suits a boat trip that mixes cliffs, a swim and the odd dolphin.</p>

      <h2>Which islands have more whales and dolphins?</h2><p>On paper they are close. The Madeira Whale Museum lists 28 species confirmed in Madeira's waters, 7 baleen whales and 21 toothed whales and dolphins. Visit Azores, the official tourist board, speaks of more than 20 kinds of cetaceans around its nine islands, and some guides count up to 28 once rare sightings are included.</p><p>The real difference is not the list but the regulars. The Azores are famous for sperm whales, the deep diving giants that hunt squid far below the surface, and for the great baleen whales that pass through in spring. Madeira's best known regulars are dolphins, especially bottlenose dolphins, which stay all year, and short-finned pilot whales, some of which are resident: researchers have photographed the same animal again after 14 years.</p><h2>When is each one at its best?</h2><p>Spring in the Azores, and any month in Madeira, with different species through the year. The table gathers what the regional sources say. None of it is a promise, because these are wild animals in an open ocean.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Species</th><th>Azores</th><th>Madeira</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sperm whale</td><td>All year</td><td>Seen frequently, according to IFCN</td></tr><tr><td>Blue, fin and sei whales</td><td>Best from March to May, some in June</td><td>Fin whales regular on migration; fin, sei and Bryde's whales seen feeding</td></tr><tr><td>Short-finned pilot whale</td><td>Not a headline species</td><td>Resident groups plus visitors</td></tr><tr><td>Bottlenose dolphin</td><td>All year</td><td>All year</td></tr><tr><td>Common dolphin</td><td>All year</td><td>Mostly winter and spring</td></tr><tr><td>Atlantic spotted dolphin</td><td>About May to September</td><td>Late spring to autumn</td></tr></tbody></table><p>So if one sight matters more than any other, such as a blue whale, plan for the Azores in spring. If you want a good chance of dolphins whenever your holiday falls, both work, and Madeira's winter is milder.</p><h2>How do the boats find the animals?</h2><p>In the Azores, often with help from the land. The vigias, lookout posts on headlands that once guided whaling crews, are still used: a spotter with powerful binoculars scans the sea and radios the boats. Whaling there ended in the 1980s, and the last catch is usually dated to 1987, off Lajes do Pico.</p><p>Madeira has the same past. Crews and gear came from the Azores in 1940, the first catch came on 2 February 1941, near the island's north west tip at Porto Moniz, and the hunt, later run from a factory at Caniçal, ended by choice in 1981. Today Madeira's whale watching boats usually rely on spotters on board, and at least one operator also uses a lookout on land. The Museu da Baleia in Caniçal tells the whole story, from harpoons to research, and it is worth the drive east of Funchal.</p><h2>What is the weather like for a day at sea?</h2><p>Madeira is a little warmer in winter and similar in summer. IPMA's 1991 to 2020 averages give Ponta Delgada, on São Miguel, a January high of 17.5 °C and an August high of 26.0 °C. Funchal's January high is about 20 °C and its August high about 26.8 °C.</p><p>The water tells a bigger story. Madeira's sea is coolest in February and March, near 18 °C, and peaks near 24 °C between August and October. Around the Azores it drops to roughly 16 °C in March and reaches about 23 °C in August, so a spring whale trip there is a jacket outing, not a swimming one. The Azores also sit far out in the open Atlantic, in the path of passing weather, so leave spare days in your plan whichever islands you choose.</p><h2>How far apart are they, and how long to get there?</h2><p>Funchal and Ponta Delgada are close to 1,000 km apart, and Azores Airlines flies between them all year, so a holiday that combines both is possible. From Lisbon, Madeira is about an hour and a half by air.</p><p>Inside each archipelago, distances work differently. Madeira is one main island with Porto Santo beside it, so a boat trip from Funchal needs no flight or ferry first. The Azores are nine islands spread over more than 600 km. The main whale watching towns, Ponta Delgada and Vila Franca do Campo on São Miguel, Lajes do Pico and Madalena on Pico, and Horta on Faial, are on three different islands, so the island you sleep on shapes the trips you can take.</p><h2>What does Madeira do better at sea?</h2><p>The coast. Few places put a cliff of about 580 metres, a fishing harbour and a swim stop in clear deep water within a few kilometres of a capital city. That is the stretch Chifbay sails from Marina do Funchal: a private boat for up to 5 guests, either the <a href="/hidden-coves-half-day">Day Trip</a>, with swims below the cliffs and paddle boards to borrow, or the <a href="/sunset-cruise">Sunset Trip</a> along the same cliffs in the evening light.</p><p>Dolphins sometimes cross that route, and the law holds every boat, ours included, to 50 metres from them. But Chifbay promises no sightings and does not run whale watching. If whales are the reason for your trip, book a dedicated tour, in Madeira or in the Azores. Our comparison of <a href="/posts/whale-watching-tour-or-private-boat-madeira">a search trip and a coastal private trip</a> helps you choose, and our guide to <a href="/posts/dolphins-and-whales-in-madeira-guide">dolphins and whales in Madeira</a> covers the local species in more depth.</p><h2>So which should you choose?</h2><ul><li><strong>Choose the Azores</strong> if seeing a sperm whale or a blue whale is the dream, if you travel in spring, and if cool water and changeable skies do not bother you.</li><li><strong>Choose Madeira</strong> if you want warmth in winter, dolphins as a likely bonus rather than the only goal, and boat days that end with a swim under the cliffs.</li><li><strong>Choose both</strong> if you have two weeks and want each archipelago at its best: whales in the Azores, coast and sea swims in Madeira.</li></ul><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>When is the best time to see blue whales in the Azores?</h3><p>Mostly from March to May, with some still passing in June, as the great baleen whales migrate through the islands.</p><h3>Can you see sperm whales in Madeira?</h3><p>Yes. IFCN describes them as seen frequently in Madeira's waters, but the Azores are better known for them, with sperm whales present all year.</p><h3>Are there direct flights between Madeira and the Azores?</h3><p>Yes. Azores Airlines flies between Funchal and Ponta Delgada all year. The two cities are close to 1,000 km apart.</p><h3>Is the sea warmer in Madeira or the Azores?</h3><p>Madeira's is warmer. It rarely falls much below 18 °C, while the sea around the Azores drops to roughly 16 °C in March.</p><h3>Did Madeira hunt whales like the Azores?</h3><p>Yes. Madeira hunted sperm whales from 1941 until 1981, using crews and gear brought over from the Azores. The Whale Museum in Caniçal tells that story.</p>

      <blockquote>The Azores are where the whales come to you. Madeira is where the cliffs do.</blockquote>
    </article>
    <p class="pref-src" data-pref-source><span>Enjoying our Madeira guides?</span> <a href="https://www.google.com/preferences/source?q=chifbay.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="#4285F4" d="M23.5 12.3c0-.8-.1-1.6-.2-2.3H12v4.5h6.5a5.6 5.6 0 0 1-2.4 3.6v3h3.9c2.2-2.1 3.5-5.1 3.5-8.8z"/><path fill="#34A853" d="M12 24c3.2 0 6-1.1 7.9-2.9l-3.9-3c-1.1.7-2.4 1.2-4 1.2-3.1 0-5.7-2.1-6.6-4.9h-4v3.1A12 12 0 0 0 12 24z"/><path fill="#FBBC05" d="M5.4 14.4a7.2 7.2 0 0 1 0-4.7V6.6h-4a12 12 0 0 0 0 10.8l4-3z"/><path fill="#EA4335" d="M12 4.8c1.7 0 3.3.6 4.6 1.8l3.4-3.4A12 12 0 0 0 1.4 6.6l4 3.1C6.3 6.9 8.9 4.8 12 4.8z"/></svg>Add Chifbay as a preferred source on Google</a></p>

